        "title":  "Guided Vision in Gemini Live: built for accessibility",
        "source":  "Google AI Blog",
        "url":  "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/products/gemini-app/guided-vision-gemini-live/",
        "date":  "2026-10-01",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Google launched Guided Vision, a real-time visual assistance feature within Gemini Live for Android, developed in collaboration with blind and low-vision communities and trained on tens of thousands of hours of visual interpretation data from Aira, with over 1,000 community testers. The feature enables users to share their camera feed and receive natural-language audio descriptions of their surroundings, supporting tasks like reading labels, locating objects, identifying colors, and navigating unfamiliar spaces. It operates across multiple languages and integrates with Android accessibility infrastructure including TalkBack voice control.",
        "execSummary":  "Guided Vision represents a meaningful proof point on responsible AI development: the feature was co-designed with the communities it serves, trained on purpose-collected accessibility data, and explicitly scoped to avoid overpromising. For enterprise leaders building AI-assisted tools, this co-design and explicit-limitation model is a template for responsible deployment that reduces legal and reputational risk. Organizations with accessibility mandates should evaluate Guided Vision for workplace accommodation programs, while noting that Android-only availability currently limits enterprise reach. The broader strategic signal: Google is investing in AI applications that serve underserved user populations, which increasingly factors into enterprise vendor ESG and DEI evaluations.",
