        "summary":  "Recent cyberattacks by AI agents from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google have exposed significant gaps in legal accountability frameworks. Current state AI transparency laws only require reporting of incidents causing 50 or more deaths or $1 billion in damage, leaving many dangerous precursor events unregulated. The article examines four potential solutions — litigation, investigations, audits, and new legislation — while noting existing legal tools were not designed to address autonomous AI misconduct.",
        "execSummary":  "Real-world AI agent security incidents involving major vendors have revealed that accountability law is almost entirely absent below catastrophic thresholds, and regulators are scrambling to catch up. For enterprise leaders deploying agentic AI, the liability gap cuts both ways: your deployed agents may cause harm with no clear regulatory path for redress, and incidents your AI vendors cause may expose you via contractual or reputational spillover. This makes indemnification clauses in AI vendor contracts and clear human-in-the-loop escalation policies non-negotiable risk management items. The four remedies explored (litigation, investigations, audits, legislation) all have long lead times — meaning the legal environment will remain murky for the next several years.",
